{"title": "Forensic geology", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Forensic geology", "definition": {"text": "The study of earth materials as evidence to answer questions raised by the legal system.", "sent_ids": ["72e0d0d09509"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Its main use today is trace evidence: soil and sediment particles can link a suspect to a crime or crime scene.", "sent_ids": ["c45f8f95296d", "30a80edfa4db"]}, {"text": "Trace evidence rests on Edmond Locard's exchange principle, that every contact leaves a trace.", "sent_ids": ["1995a217e446"]}, {"text": "In 1891 Hans Gross used microscopic analysis of soil from a suspect's shoes to link him to a crime scene.", "sent_ids": ["13cf58fb93a6"]}, {"text": "Ray Murray dates the field's idea to Arthur Conan Doyle, whose Sherlock Holmes could tell where someone had been from clays on their shoes.", "sent_ids": ["44db12c3a1b1", "63f095431e88"]}, {"text": "Murray and John Tedrow published the book Forensic Geology in 1975.", "sent_ids": ["4dd6c13c6990"]}, {"text": "Ground-penetrating radar is used mainly to find buried bodies, narrowing the search area and cutting excavation time.", "sent_ids": ["2f7ee13e367f", "a579ea966912"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-077"], "related": ["soil-hist-085", "soil-hist-086", "soil-hist-087"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "events": [{"date_text": "1891", "sent_ids": ["13cf58fb93a6"]}, {"date_text": "1975", "sent_ids": ["4dd6c13c6990"]}], "mention_sent_ids": ["98c1b7784464", "31c02f05e222"], "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-084", "score": 12.08, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 3, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 4.5}, "selected": true, "unit": 558, "position": 3, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Forensic geology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forensic_geology", "revid": 1384888744, "sent_ids": ["c45f8f95296d", "30a80edfa4db", "13cf58fb93a6", "44db12c3a1b1", "63f095431e88", "4dd6c13c6990", "2f7ee13e367f", "a579ea966912", "72e0d0d09509", "98c1b7784464", "31c02f05e222"], "key": "Forensic geology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1384888744"}, {"title": "Trace evidence", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trace_evidence", "revid": 1383245470, "sent_ids": ["1995a217e446"], "key": "Trace evidence", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1383245470"}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3760330", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-077"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-077": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Forensic soil comparison", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Forensic geology#Physical description", "definition": {"text": "The comparison of a questioned soil sample of unknown origin with control samples, such as soil from the crime scene, to find similarities or differences.", "sent_ids": ["dfa638610f9e", "52cc86a557b6", "2b06297b9fee", "3d97f14dbae8"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Colour is one of the most important properties; it is matched to the Munsell soil chart, though colour perception differs between people.", "sent_ids": ["1536cddd40db", "edd98842613f", "aafa891d5c7f"]}, {"text": "Spectrophotometry using CIELAB coordinates gives objective numerical colour values to avoid errors of human perception.", "sent_ids": ["c0446d722d82", "d8ed39560eca"]}, {"text": "Particle-size distribution is among the most discriminating properties, measured by microscope, laser diffraction or dry or wet sieving.", "sent_ids": ["d8dd0e1c1f9e", "5891dce79294"]}, {"text": "pH can point to elements such as P, Zn, B, Cu and Fe and to lime requirement.", "sent_ids": ["c09f77aeda1c"]}, {"text": "Because soils differ over very short distances, the questioned sample is examined first to choose where to sample at the scene.", "sent_ids": ["b43b8e1cd6f9"]}, {"text": "A lump of soil is kept whole to preserve its layers, and ground samples are taken from different horizons varying in colour, minerals and texture.", "sent_ids": ["773d571492d9", "1a26345b9e59", "9f15e92ea5d3"]}, {"text": "Samples are dried before collection, or refrigerated if damp, so microbial activity does not change their composition.", "sent_ids": ["c8f47d110bd0", "a4f319891710"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-084", "soil-found-025", "soil-phys-018"], "related": ["soil-found-026", "soil-found-014"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Forensic geology"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-085", "score": 5.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 558, "position": 4, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Forensic geology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forensic_geology", "revid": 1384888744, "sent_ids": ["1536cddd40db", "edd98842613f", "aafa891d5c7f", "c0446d722d82", "d8ed39560eca", "d8dd0e1c1f9e", "5891dce79294", "c09f77aeda1c", "b43b8e1cd6f9", "773d571492d9", "1a26345b9e59", "9f15e92ea5d3", "c8f47d110bd0", "a4f319891710", "dfa638610f9e", "52cc86a557b6", "2b06297b9fee", "3d97f14dbae8"], "key": "Forensic geology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1384888744"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-hist-084", "soil-found-025", "soil-phys-018"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-084": "ordering pass", "soil-found-025": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-018": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Forensic palynology", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Forensic palynology", "definition": {"text": "The use of pollen, spores and other palynomorphs to prove or disprove a link between objects, people and places in criminal and civil cases.", "sent_ids": ["00476c7a1821"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Regions, and even parts of one garden, have distinctive pollen assemblages, and pollen can also show the season it was picked up.", "sent_ids": ["2d95410758e3", "d267ff8438ed"]}, {"text": "Pollen in soil, clothing, hair and rock is hard for a suspect to remove because it adheres so well.", "sent_ids": ["2b3507cdb6d2"]}, {"text": "In a 2023 Argentine case, soil in the suspect's car and shoe soles matched the palynomorph composition of the coast where the body was found.", "sent_ids": ["3485ea79b333"]}, {"text": "A Bolivian study built a forensic database of pollen assemblages in high-altitude, non-seasonal soils.", "sent_ids": ["55deecb15d84", "71a20ab6ac0f"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-132", "soil-hist-084"], "related": ["soil-hist-085"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-086", "score": 3.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 559, "position": 2, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Forensic palynology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forensic_palynology", "revid": 1377287459, "sent_ids": ["2d95410758e3", "d267ff8438ed", "2b3507cdb6d2", "3485ea79b333", "55deecb15d84", "71a20ab6ac0f", "00476c7a1821"], "key": "Forensic palynology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1377287459"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q5468724", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-hist-132", "soil-hist-084"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-132": "ordering pass", "soil-hist-084": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Forensic archaeology", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Forensic anthropology#Forensic archaeology", "definition": {"text": "The branch of forensic anthropology that uses archaeological methods in legal cases, such as finding overlooked graves and processing mass graves.", "sent_ids": ["51e9103ba7e8", "305e810d123f", "5d3ad4594827"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Differences in the soil help forensic archaeologists locate grave sites.", "sent_ids": ["00ca39869005"]}, {"text": "Burying a body leaves a small mound of soil from the grave fill.", "sent_ids": ["8519f2510962"]}, {"text": "Loose soil and nutrients from the decomposing body encourage different plant growth from the surroundings.", "sent_ids": ["25649def18d4"]}, {"text": "Grave sites typically have looser, darker, more organic soil than the area around them.", "sent_ids": ["ec851a0f22ad"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-084", "soil-hist-095"], "related": ["soil-hist-089", "soil-hist-091"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-087", "score": 4.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 559, "position": 3, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Forensic anthropology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forensic_anthropology", "revid": 1384555902, "sent_ids": ["00ca39869005", "8519f2510962", "25649def18d4", "ec851a0f22ad", "51e9103ba7e8", "305e810d123f", "5d3ad4594827"], "key": "Forensic anthropology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1384555902"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-hist-084"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-084": "ordering pass", "soil-hist-095": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Taphonomy", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Taphonomy", "definition": {"text": "The study of how organisms decay and become fossilized or preserved.", "sent_ids": ["22b999ef09fc"]}, "facts": [{"text": "The Soviet scientist Ivan Efremov introduced the term in 1940 for the passage of remains from the biosphere to the lithosphere.", "sent_ids": ["d998f10b2847"]}, {"text": "Its processes fall into biostratinomy, between death and burial, and diagenesis, after burial.", "sent_ids": ["7bd1031177f2"]}, {"text": "In archaeology it considers effects such as burning, rodent disturbance, gnaw marks and soil pH.", "sent_ids": ["f5f9de98b380"]}, {"text": "Archaeologists observe these transformation processes to understand their impact on archaeological material at real sites.", "sent_ids": ["7949b8fa5418"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-115"], "related": ["soil-hist-089", "soil-hist-102"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-088", "score": 7.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.892, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "unit": 557, "position": 1, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Taphonomy", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taphonomy", "revid": 1374015200, "sent_ids": ["d998f10b2847", "7bd1031177f2", "f5f9de98b380", "7949b8fa5418", "22b999ef09fc"], "key": "Taphonomy", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1374015200"}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1940", "date_ma": 9.999999999999999e-06, "sent_ids": ["d998f10b2847"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q596086", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-115"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-115": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Geotaphonomy", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Forensic anthropology#Forensic taphonomy", "definition": {"text": "The study of how a decomposing body and its burial affect the surrounding environment.", "sent_ids": ["5b87d66529f5", "ee394967e5c3"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It examines how the soil was disturbed, how the pH around the body changed, and whether plant growth sped up or slowed.", "sent_ids": ["247c72d05d42"]}, {"text": "It also covers tool marks from digging the grave and changes to land and drainage from adding an unnatural mass.", "sent_ids": ["0571b01d5a1c"]}, {"text": "Forensic taphonomy as a whole studies post-mortem changes to remains caused by soil, water, plants, insects and animals.", "sent_ids": ["9d769bb25bd0"]}, {"text": "William M. Bass created the first body farm to study decomposition in conditions such as partial or full burial.", "sent_ids": ["c8bbae4f6816"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-088", "soil-bio-115", "soil-found-014"], "related": ["soil-hist-087"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "mention_sent_ids": ["affa434c5223"], "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-089", "score": 5.78, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 559, "position": 4, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Forensic anthropology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forensic_anthropology", "revid": 1384555902, "sent_ids": ["247c72d05d42", "9d769bb25bd0", "c8bbae4f6816", "5b87d66529f5", "affa434c5223"], "key": "Forensic anthropology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1384555902"}, {"title": "Taphonomy", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taphonomy", "revid": 1374015200, "sent_ids": ["0571b01d5a1c", "ee394967e5c3"], "key": "Taphonomy", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1374015200"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-hist-088", "soil-found-014"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-088": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-115": "ordering pass", "soil-found-014": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Environmental DNA", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Environmental DNA", "definition": {"text": "DNA collected from environmental samples such as soil, sediment, water, snow or air rather than from an individual organism.", "sent_ids": ["22c2f3a05949"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Sequencing it reveals which species are present in an ecosystem, including microscopic ones not otherwise detectable.", "sent_ids": ["1cc9a8aeb027"]}, {"text": "Many microbes cannot be cultured in the laboratory, so their genetic make-up is accessible only through eDNA.", "sent_ids": ["24409512a21b", "27917be10e3e"]}, {"text": "Extracellular DNA can reach 2 μg/L in soil and 88 μg/L in natural waters.", "sent_ids": ["770960b380f8"]}, {"text": "It was first applied to terrestrial sediments, yielding DNA of extinct and living animals and plants, known as sedimentary ancient DNA or 'dirtDNA'.", "sent_ids": ["e51f71a0f1e4", "9520cfd8e781"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-011", "soil-bio-221"], "related": ["soil-hist-085"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-090", "score": 5.22, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.219, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 557, "position": 3, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Environmental DNA", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_DNA", "revid": 1369638516, "sent_ids": ["1cc9a8aeb027", "24409512a21b", "27917be10e3e", "770960b380f8", "e51f71a0f1e4", "9520cfd8e781", "22c2f3a05949"], "key": "Environmental DNA", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1369638516"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q25098939", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-011"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-011": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-221": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Geophysical survey (archaeology)", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Geophysical survey (archaeology)", "definition": {"text": "Ground-based physical sensing techniques used to image or map buried archaeological features.", "sent_ids": ["86cdfcc57561"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Instruments detect buried features whose physical properties contrast measurably with their surroundings, without digging or damage.", "sent_ids": ["9e31ea85f3be", "88b067ce9e60"]}, {"text": "Most sites lie within the top metre, and targets may be as faint as organic staining from decayed wooden posts.", "sent_ids": ["be39ba343526", "cd0dbab55ac6"]}, {"text": "The usual instruments are magnetometers, electrical resistance meters, ground-penetrating radar and electromagnetic conductivity meters.", "sent_ids": ["e0c93eb13b57"]}, {"text": "A stone foundation resists electric current, while organic midden deposits conduct it more easily than surrounding soils.", "sent_ids": ["2742841ef42b"]}, {"text": "Magnetometers respond strongly to brick and burned soil and can pick up subtle anomalies from disturbed soils or decayed organic matter.", "sent_ids": ["075bb5bbe070", "1c8d6c2a1665"]}, {"text": "Radar works best in uniform sandy soils; conductive clays and silts weaken the signal and rocky sediments scatter it.", "sent_ids": ["c7ce53bb293d", "92f8a469b525"]}, {"text": "Ploughed fields are a source of magnetic noise, while old tracks can show through differential compaction in magnetic soils.", "sent_ids": ["3510785af4e3", "c6dd2cc820aa"]}, {"text": "The approach was pioneered in Britain in the 1940s and 1950s.", "sent_ids": ["4a725a41390b"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-meth-014", "soil-meth-015", "soil-hist-093", "soil-phys-042"], "related": ["soil-hist-092", "soil-hist-084", "soil-hist-102"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-091", "score": 7.97, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 560, "position": 1, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Geophysical survey (archaeology)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geophysical_survey_(archaeology)", "revid": 1385102583, "sent_ids": ["9e31ea85f3be", "88b067ce9e60", "be39ba343526", "cd0dbab55ac6", "e0c93eb13b57", "2742841ef42b", "075bb5bbe070", "1c8d6c2a1665", "c7ce53bb293d", "92f8a469b525", "4a725a41390b", "86cdfcc57561"], "key": "Geophysical survey (archaeology)", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1385102583"}, {"title": "Magnetometer", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetometer", "revid": 1383691314, "sent_ids": ["3510785af4e3", "c6dd2cc820aa"], "key": "Magnetometer", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1383691314"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3610005", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-phys-042", "soil-hist-093"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-meth-014": "ordering pass", "soil-meth-015": "ordering pass", "soil-hist-093": "ordering pass", "soil-phys-042": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Geoarchaeology", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Geoarchaeology", "definition": {"text": "A multidisciplinary field that applies the theories and techniques of the geosciences to archaeology.", "sent_ids": ["6639fe3cf3fa"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It draws on geomorphology, sedimentology, pedology, stratigraphy and geochronology to interpret the sediments, soils and landforms of sites.", "sent_ids": ["6eb17b783541"]}, {"text": "Its three aims are to place sites in time, to understand the natural processes that create them, and to reconstruct their landscapes.", "sent_ids": ["f17be299da44", "052690f429a7", "3b53f2015116", "3418d0f92ea6"]}, {"text": "It studies natural processes that affect buried sites and artefacts after deposition.", "sent_ids": ["3b94f3562ea3"]}, {"text": "Its stratigraphic principles go back to Nicolas Steno in 1669 and James Hutton in 1788; it became a subfield of archaeology in the 1970s–1980s.", "sent_ids": ["98f60bf2d785", "a14da8ef6c64"]}, {"text": "Monolith sampling hammers narrow metal tins into a section in overlapping sequence to collect a site's complete profile for laboratory study.", "sent_ids": ["15a590b435ed", "b4d9a1e64bf6", "d4ee90debbd4"]}, {"text": "A soil sample's particle-size distribution may show the conditions under which its layer was deposited.", "sent_ids": ["970ebf51d900"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-095", "soil-water-295", "soil-water-046", "soil-found-077"], "related": ["soil-meth-019", "soil-hist-093", "soil-hist-094", "soil-hist-095", "soil-form-020"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Magnetic susceptibility analysis", "Soil phosphate survey"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-092", "score": 10.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 3.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 557, "position": 2, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Geoarchaeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoarchaeology", "revid": 1369614590, "sent_ids": ["6eb17b783541", "f17be299da44", "052690f429a7", "3b53f2015116", "3418d0f92ea6", "3b94f3562ea3", "98f60bf2d785", "a14da8ef6c64", "15a590b435ed", "b4d9a1e64bf6", "d4ee90debbd4", "970ebf51d900", "6639fe3cf3fa"], "key": "Geoarchaeology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1369614590"}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1669", "date_ma": 0.000281, "sent_ids": ["98f60bf2d785"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q431602", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-077", "soil-found-095"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-095": "ordering pass", "soil-water-295": "ordering pass", "soil-water-046": "ordering pass", "soil-found-077": "consolidation"}}
{"title": "Magnetic susceptibility analysis", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Geoarchaeology#Magnetic Susceptibility Analysis", "definition": {"text": "Measurement of how readily a material becomes magnetised in an external magnetic field, used on soils and sediments.", "sent_ids": ["19137329dd55"]}, "facts": [{"text": "A soil's susceptibility reflects magnetic iron oxides such as maghemite, not its total iron content.", "sent_ids": ["66bfceff3e0e", "9d590099024f"]}, {"text": "Burning and microbial activity form magnetic iron, as in topsoils and some anaerobic deposits.", "sent_ids": ["0a7d077207c5"]}, {"text": "It finds hearths and burning residues and tells reddening by fire from reddening by natural processes such as gleying.", "sent_ids": ["c632d6ede435", "a964d6d25d8f"]}, {"text": "It can also identify buried soils and soil material redeposited in peat or lake sediments.", "sent_ids": ["bd98ff84e381", "48ad3521f845"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-chem-163", "soil-form-042"], "related": ["soil-hist-091", "soil-class-127"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Geoarchaeology", "Soil phosphate survey"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-093", "score": 4.89, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "unit": 559, "position": 5, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Geoarchaeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoarchaeology", "revid": 1369614590, "sent_ids": ["66bfceff3e0e", "9d590099024f", "0a7d077207c5", "c632d6ede435", "a964d6d25d8f", "bd98ff84e381", "48ad3521f845", "19137329dd55"], "key": "Geoarchaeology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1369614590"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-chem-163", "soil-form-042"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-chem-163": "ordering pass", "soil-form-042": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Soil phosphate survey", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Geoarchaeology#Phosphate and Orthophosphate Content with Spectrophotometry", "definition": {"text": "Measuring and mapping phosphate in soil, which comes from people, their animals, rubbish and bones, to find former activity areas and sites.", "sent_ids": ["40f4202c03ad", "ea4e2b5f2f40"]}, "facts": [{"text": "One hundred people excrete about 62 kg of phosphate a year, with about as much again from their rubbish.", "sent_ids": ["dd7d56c11c6b"]}, {"text": "Most of it is quickly fixed on soil clay and can persist for thousands of years, so human activity soon makes large differences.", "sent_ids": ["2baee1876d55", "76c264999d43"]}, {"text": "The human body holds about 650 g of phosphate, raising levels at burial sites.", "sent_ids": ["dbb7c689509e"]}, {"text": "Phosphorus is detected by the molybdate blue reaction, whose colour depth is proportional to concentration.", "sent_ids": ["915e662cdceb"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-fert-127", "soil-hist-092"], "related": ["soil-hist-102", "soil-class-057"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Geoarchaeology", "Magnetic susceptibility analysis"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "mention_sent_ids": ["9d3722af1b60"], "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-094", "score": 3.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 560, "position": 2, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Geoarchaeology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoarchaeology", "revid": 1369614590, "sent_ids": ["dd7d56c11c6b", "2baee1876d55", "76c264999d43", "dbb7c689509e", "915e662cdceb", "40f4202c03ad", "ea4e2b5f2f40", "9d3722af1b60"], "key": "Geoarchaeology", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1369614590"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-fert-127", "soil-hist-092"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-fert-127": "ordering pass", "soil-hist-092": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Stratigraphy (archaeology)", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Stratigraphy (archaeology)", "definition": {"text": "The study of a site's sequence of superimposed units, or contexts, to learn when and how its deposits formed.", "sent_ids": ["5f13bd355650", "bc392ddbc3ed"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Contexts are single events that leave discrete traces: deposits such as a ditch's back-fill, structures such as walls, or 'cuts' that remove earlier material.", "sent_ids": ["15d6f78f16cd", "1837d9e64b4e", "763aa2caa448"]}, {"text": "Stratigraphic relationships give the order in which contexts formed: a ditch must be cut before it is back-filled.", "sent_ids": ["fe41c63190cb", "22136962a738"]}, {"text": "A Harris matrix represents a site's formation in space and time.", "sent_ids": ["ed3b86afdd55"]}, {"text": "Two long-accepted principles: lower units are older than upper ones, and a layer is no older than the most recent artefact in it.", "sent_ids": ["e074a3de10f9", "16d1ca2bba28"]}, {"text": "E.C. Harris added the principles of original horizontality, lateral continuity and stratigraphic succession.", "sent_ids": ["bfc0435f7d24", "cff4b2d17674", "3260794920a3", "8a06857cc19f"]}, {"text": "A context sealed between two dated layers must date between them.", "sent_ids": ["35e34b0b0392"]}, {"text": "Older 'residual' finds and later 'intrusive' finds can enter a context, so dating rests on its latest evidence.", "sent_ids": ["1c00a9e42ef6", "1b2ed9fbb805", "3d0d57db4a40"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-096", "soil-found-001"], "related": ["soil-hist-092", "soil-hist-102"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-095", "score": 5.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 558, "position": 2, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Stratigraphy (archaeology)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stratigraphy_(archaeology)", "revid": 1365213834, "sent_ids": ["15d6f78f16cd", "1837d9e64b4e", "763aa2caa448", "fe41c63190cb", "22136962a738", "ed3b86afdd55", "e074a3de10f9", "16d1ca2bba28", "bfc0435f7d24", "cff4b2d17674", "3260794920a3", "8a06857cc19f", "35e34b0b0392", "1c00a9e42ef6", "1b2ed9fbb805", "3d0d57db4a40", "5f13bd355650", "bc392ddbc3ed"], "key": "Stratigraphy (archaeology)", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1365213834"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q1456659", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-hist-096", "soil-found-001"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-096": "ordering pass", "soil-found-001": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Law of superposition", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Foundational", "article": "Law of superposition", "definition": {"text": "The principle that in undeformed layered sequences the oldest layers lie at the bottom and newer material stacks on top.", "sent_ids": ["6203a48a6ab7"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Nicolas Steno first proposed it in 1669, and William 'Strata' Smith popularized it in English to make the first geologic map of Britain.", "sent_ids": ["1fc69a6fd5a9", "d438df999df8"]}, {"text": "Archaeological layers can form by undercutting earlier ones, so applying superposition at sites needs interpretation.", "sent_ids": ["1d92184352e9", "801128f229d0"]}, {"text": "Animals, vegetation and limestone crystallization can disrupt the original layering.", "sent_ids": ["9c99c8d046d2"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-form-136"], "related": ["soil-hist-095", "soil-hist-119", "soil-found-001"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "events": [{"date_text": "1669", "sent_ids": ["1fc69a6fd5a9"]}], "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-096", "score": 5.7, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "unit": 558, "position": 1, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Law of superposition", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law_of_superposition", "revid": 1377409560, "sent_ids": ["1fc69a6fd5a9", "d438df999df8", "1d92184352e9", "801128f229d0", "9c99c8d046d2", "6203a48a6ab7"], "key": "Law of superposition", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1377409560"}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q842427", "prerequisites_draft": [], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-form-136": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Cropmark", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Cropmark", "definition": {"text": "A pattern in growing crops, visible from the air or high ground, that reveals buried archaeological, natural or recent features.", "sent_ids": ["bbf2f1f5381b"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Cropmarks come from differential growth, and soil conditions are a key factor.", "sent_ids": ["a999367de7af", "f25fd19c211a"]}, {"text": "A buried wall diverts water and displaces fertile soil, stunting crops, while an organic-rich ditch fill holds water and boosts growth.", "sent_ids": ["0c515275adc8", "bffccbacf986"]}, {"text": "They show only seasonally and may need very wet or dry years; droughts make them appear even in hardy grass.", "sent_ids": ["0a881e579664", "f2093cb03ebd"]}, {"text": "Thermal imaging can show crop marks at any growth stage, because water loss depends on water available to roots.", "sent_ids": ["3dda92f1880e"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-hist-098"], "related": ["soil-hist-098", "soil-hist-091"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-097", "score": 2.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 560, "position": 3, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Cropmark", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cropmark", "revid": 1379426515, "sent_ids": ["a999367de7af", "f25fd19c211a", "0c515275adc8", "bffccbacf986", "0a881e579664", "f2093cb03ebd", "3dda92f1880e", "bbf2f1f5381b"], "key": "Cropmark", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1379426515"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q852811", "prerequisites_draft": [], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-hist-098": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Soil mark", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Foundational", "article": "Soil mark", "definition": {"text": "A difference in soil colour caused by archaeological features beneath it.", "sent_ids": ["d2064b6e9cf5"]}, "facts": [{"text": "A ploughed-out earthwork leaves hard, dry bank material and damper ditch material, or a feature may cut through topsoil to expose chalk.", "sent_ids": ["697a72ce0498", "084a31d1eadf"]}, {"text": "They usually show in ploughed or harrowed fields before crops grow.", "sent_ids": ["45bab09a4b16"]}, {"text": "Black or red soil can show organic or burnt deposits, indicating whether fire was used.", "sent_ids": ["8ef64f765ec0", "1560385eccd7"]}, {"text": "They can reveal former river channels or slightly raised dry ground on floodplains that attracted settlement.", "sent_ids": ["1dc1b6aba3dc"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-025"], "related": ["soil-hist-097", "soil-hist-099"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-098", "score": 4.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 557, "position": 4, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Soil mark", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_mark", "revid": 1280615291, "sent_ids": ["697a72ce0498", "084a31d1eadf", "45bab09a4b16", "8ef64f765ec0", "1560385eccd7", "1dc1b6aba3dc", "d2064b6e9cf5"], "key": "Soil mark", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1280615291"}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3964128", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-025"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-025": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Celtic field", "kind": "Practice", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Foundational", "article": "Celtic field", "definition": {"text": "An old name for traces of prehistoric field systems in north-west Europe.", "sent_ids": ["d8d5dfbeadf7"]}, "facts": [{"text": "The fields have nothing to do with Celtic culture; O. G. S. Crawford gave them the name.", "sent_ids": ["382f1413f61b", "b481979da5c9"]}, {"text": "They date from the Early Bronze Age (c. 1800 BC) to the early medieval period and survive as earthworks or soil marks.", "sent_ids": ["8a0a947b1156", "596512037f64"]}, {"text": "Plots were rarely more than 2,000 m², small enough for one person or household to cultivate.", "sent_ids": ["82ba9e6d59ba", "c157bd980be1"]}, {"text": "Lynchets at their upper and lower ends are evidence of early ploughing.", "sent_ids": ["d76b8421768d"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-agri-106", "soil-agri-081"], "related": ["soil-agri-081", "soil-hist-098", "soil-hist-100"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-099", "score": 3.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 558, "position": 5, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Celtic field", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celtic_field", "revid": 1143580835, "sent_ids": ["382f1413f61b", "b481979da5c9", "8a0a947b1156", "596512037f64", "82ba9e6d59ba", "c157bd980be1", "d76b8421768d", "d8d5dfbeadf7"], "key": "Celtic field", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1143580835"}], "events": [{"date_text": "c. 1800 BC", "date_ma": 0.00375, "sent_ids": ["8a0a947b1156"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q1053214", "prerequisites_draft": [], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-agri-106": "ordering pass", "soil-agri-081": "ordering pass"}}
{"title": "Ridge and furrow", "kind": "Practice", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Foundational", "article": "Ridge and furrow", "definition": {"text": "A pattern of ridges and troughs left by a ploughing system used in medieval Europe, typical of the open-field system.", "sent_ids": ["25311145578e"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Non-reversible ploughs turned soil toward the centre of the same strip each year, gradually building a ridge with a furrow between strips.", "sent_ids": ["c5bad3361e6f", "0b58aeefc75a", "494256626245"]}, {"text": "Ridges drained wet soils into the furrows; only some well-drained soils were left flat.", "sent_ids": ["117cd2241726", "7b4fed09d930"]}, {"text": "Surviving ridges stand up to 24 inches (61 cm), but in use they were over 6 feet (1.8 m) in places.", "sent_ids": ["ae6bb73145b9", "1aec49e4f813"]}, {"text": "The pattern survives where land became grassland and was never ploughed again; reversible ploughs erased it elsewhere.", "sent_ids": ["b0b49c9607cb", "036c89389724"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-agri-106"], "related": ["soil-agri-109", "soil-agri-081", "soil-hist-099", "soil-agri-128"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "History, people and frontiers", "chapter": "Forensic and archaeological soil science", "id": "soil-hist-100", "score": 3.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "unit": 559, "position": 1, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Ridge and furrow", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_and_furrow", "revid": 1372866300, "sent_ids": ["c5bad3361e6f", "0b58aeefc75a", "494256626245", "117cd2241726", "7b4fed09d930", "ae6bb73145b9", "1aec49e4f813", "b0b49c9607cb", "036c89389724", "25311145578e"], "key": "Ridge and furrow", "source": "wikipedia", "version": "1372866300"}], "events": [